ccepted_at":"2026-11-26T00:17:13.899329+00:00","exit_code":null,"finished_at":null,"id":"e08db96d3a3f043a749e","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"accepted","target":"CHAD Bash"}],"retention_limit":48,"schema":"GCL_CONTROL_ACTIVITY_FEED/2","transcripts":[{"command":"set -o pipefail; curl -fsS --max-time 10 https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME/57f110e12df3a86bf18dab79bc970382bfe9973b/governance/erdos_proof_verification/evidence/0027/SPLIT_KERNEL_SCOPE_001.md | sed -n '1,24p'","exit_code":0,"id":"64217c41145432a084e4","policy":"GCL_AUTOMATIC_PUBLIC_SOURCE_TRANSCRIPT/1","redactions":{"command":{},"output":{}},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME","revision":"57f110e12df3a86bf18dab79bc970382bfe9973b","source_end":"2026-11-26T00:17:03.944523+00:00","stdout_stderr":"# Prime-power decomposition and original-modulus CRT fibre density\n\nErdos27Split.lean SHA256 f4851fed68f7f79a938e39155f6ca7257f1464ae8f65c58ca774886f88a64207. Twenty audited declarations; standard axioms only. Exact pinned managed SSD replay capture 8d082e266e62483fb7b65e4960af1f0e. Five source hashes and nine dependency revisions checked.\n\nFor every positive modulus and natural cutoff Q, split_exists constructs positive factors a,b by strong induction on n, removing one prime at a time, assigning it to the side selected by p <= Q. The smooth and rough predicates require every prime divisor to lie on its respective side. cross_coprime excludes a shared prime. split_unique uses coprime cancellation and divisibility antisymmetry; smooth_power_allocation and rough_power_allocation prove the entire power p^k divides the chosen factor iff it divides the product, for all k. Both predicates survive multiplication, taking divisors and finite LCM. For every finite indexed family (including empty families, repeated moduli and modulus one), family_split produces a,b, proves coprimality of M=lcm(a_i) and B=lcm(b_i), and lcm(n_i)=M*B. Positive LCM follows from positive factors. family_density transports congruence avoidance from a_i*b_i to the original n_i and applies the earlier protected CRT count identity: the density on M*B is the average over h modulo M of fibre avoidance density on B. Thus the prime decomposition is no longer an assumed hypothesis of the arithmetic CRT bridge.\n\nNo new Mathlib import needed; no external factorization theorem or extra assumption. Initial family_density elaboration errors in local NeZero synthesis and explicit rational count coercion fixed before exact successful replay. Independent regression: 32,000 modulus/cutoff pairs (n=1..1000,Q=0..31), 92,064 prime-power checks, 480 seeded finite-family CRT density identities including empty families, two hostile controls. Reference trial division compared to enumerating the maximal smooth divisor; fibre sums compared to direct avoidance counts. Digest 776eb0c99d5a87928a5ed2b27cc24e164ba9e1b0a82c7763d81156ff05855ea6; capture be7969ab289d489c8d1209da8d210455. 19 formal dependency tests and route/execution checks PASS.\n\nSource imports the already protected common-period module (#3764), not the pending density module. Branch is stacked on #3768 candidate 0bdbf11d360750cf357cd508aca7addf6cf70dfe to avoid concurrent formal-registry edits. Admit only after parent or exact equivalent protected merge. Residual: formalize fibre-pair projection and compatible CRT collision sum (Lemma 3.2), geometric mean and analytic sieve/smooth-tail estimates, real filter limit, and full Theorem B. No claim of full analytic theorem or external certification.\n"}],"withheld_candidates":0}
